1
00:00:00,395 --> 00:00:07,233
In 2023, heat killed 2,325 people in the
U.S. Texas set a record with 334 deaths,

2
00:00:07,253 --> 00:00:09,805
and Harris County had the most.

3
00:00:11,148 --> 00:00:15,991
This is HeatWise, built by Aariv Patel,
a high-school student from the Houston area.

4
00:00:19,143 --> 00:00:23,222
Weather apps say it feels like 104. But
the heat index is a shade number.

5
00:00:23,242 --> 00:00:29,235
It leaves out the sun and how hard you're working, and
OSHA warns that workers have died of heat stroke at a

6
00:00:29,255 --> 00:00:30,601
heat index of only 86.

7
00:00:30,978 --> 00:00:35,520
A coach needs to know: is practice safe
right now, and if not, when is?

8
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:41,812
Type a city, keep football practice, which uses
the Texas UIL heat flags schools must follow,

9
00:00:41,832 --> 00:00:45,894
and pick a two-hour practice between 6 AM and 9 PM.

10
00:00:47,366 --> 00:00:53,657
For U.S. places, HeatWise uses the National
Weather Service's hourly forecast and estimates WBGT,

11
00:00:53,677 --> 00:00:57,714
the heat-stress index UIL, OSHA and the military use.

12
00:00:58,084 --> 00:01:03,337
This is live for Sugar Land today. The card shows
this hour's flag and what it means for practice,

13
00:01:03,357 --> 00:01:05,835
and the safest window in the next 48 hours.

14
00:01:06,206 --> 00:01:12,183
Sunshine data didn't load for this recording, so HeatWise
used its simpler estimate and says so on screen.

15
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:16,269
On the chart, the line is heat
stress, the colored bands are the flags,

16
00:01:16,289 --> 00:01:18,404
and the green box is the best time.

17
00:01:19,864 --> 00:01:25,282
Now a known dangerous day: August 24,
2023, when Houston hit 109 degrees,

18
00:01:25,302 --> 00:01:30,721
replayed from the airport's archived reports and
labeled as history, not a forecast.

19
00:01:31,092 --> 00:01:36,575
The hottest hour hit a black flag: no
outdoor workouts. HeatWise moves practice to early morning,

20
00:01:36,595 --> 00:01:37,951
while it's still green.

21
00:01:39,654 --> 00:01:45,956
Same weather, different person. Switch to roofing, and HeatWise
uses the OSHA and NIOSH limit for heavy work.

22
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:51,619
Check acclimatized, and the limit rises
from about 73 to 79 degrees.

23
00:01:51,639 --> 00:01:54,836
It's personal to the activity, not just the weather.

24
00:01:56,297 --> 00:02:00,848
Below the chart: a water plan
from NIOSH, a day-by-day acclimatization schedule,

25
00:02:00,868 --> 00:02:04,276
and heat-stroke warning signs with call 911 up front.

26
00:02:06,037 --> 00:02:09,273
The one-page coach plan puts the whole day on one sheet,

27
00:02:09,293 --> 00:02:13,122
here in Spanish for families and crews,
ready to print for the clipboard.

28
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:17,318
And one click turns the whole app into Spanish.

29
00:02:19,896 --> 00:02:28,160
Is it right? HeatWise was checked against the National Weather
Service at eight Houston-area points over 1,472 forecast hours.

30
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:34,060
Its heat index matched the Weather Service's within 0.28 °F.

31
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:38,962
For WBGT, the average bias is about
half a degree, on the cautious side,

32
00:02:38,982 --> 00:02:44,165
and the typical gap is about 1 °F.
The first version read almost four degrees low,

33
00:02:44,185 --> 00:02:47,091
so U.S. places switched to the Weather Service grid.

34
00:02:49,071 --> 00:02:56,063
Under the hood: the Liljegren physics model for WBGT,
and a sliding-window search for the safest window.

35
00:02:56,083 --> 00:03:01,013
It's plain JavaScript, free, with no
API keys, and 84 passing tests.

36
00:03:02,086 --> 00:03:05,849
HeatWise: know the heat, and plan around it. Thanks for watching.

